			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842196">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: Uh…someone who is a transgender supporter and has a magic desk?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This just summarises your elevator pitch in less detail; when really what I want to know here is 'what is the article doing with the SCP'.</p>
<p>My concern here is that we just have a SCP that's really good at debating people who don't like trans people and there's not&#8230;really a lot you can do with that that's interesting beyond putting it into such debates and I would generally prefer to not have to see either a bunch of actual anti-trans rhetoric or a bunch of strawmans of anti-trans rhetoric.</p>
<p>This really needs some kinda narrative: it almost feels like this could be, for example, a GAW Mister (see e.g. <a href="/scp-3090">SCP-3090</a>) where they made it to like, defend trans people but it went wrong in some manner perhaps. Or perhaps the narrative is more focussed around the interactions with 946 but in a way that actually like&#8230;goes somewhere? At the moment, it feels like you're mention of that is more set dressing than impactful story because it's just kinda,,,something you've thrown out with no real exploration beyond 'they have a debate', y'know?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

